window.NEO_FIELDKIT={"unit":"algebraic-expressions","expedition":"The Lighthouse","tools":[{"title":"Substitution scroll","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"18\" y=\"20\" width=\"64\" height=\"60\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f3e6c4\" stroke=\"#8a6d40\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><circle cx=\"18\" cy=\"50\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#c9a35a\" stroke=\"#8a6d40\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"82\" cy=\"50\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#c9a35a\" stroke=\"#8a6d40\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"58\" font-size=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\" font-family=\"Georgia\" font-style=\"italic\">x→(−2)</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>Put the number in brackets wherever the letter appears.</b></p><p>If <i>x</i> = −3, then <i>x</i>² − 4<i>x</i> = (−3)² − 4 × (−3) = 9 + 12 = 21.</p><p>A negative number squared is positive. Subtracting a negative is adding.</p>","room":"The Door","lesson":"Lesson 1 · Expressions, equations, formulae and identities"},{"title":"Bracket hook","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><path d=\"M30 20 Q14 50 30 80\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#5c6670\" stroke-width=\"9\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><path d=\"M70 20 Q86 50 70 80\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#5c6670\" stroke-width=\"9\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"24\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\" font-family=\"Georgia\">×</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>Multiply every term inside the bracket.</b></p><p>4(3<i>x</i> − 2) = 12<i>x</i> − 8</p><p><b>Factorising</b> runs it backwards: find the common factor and take it outside. 12<i>x</i> − 8 = 4(3<i>x</i> − 2).</p>","room":"The Store Room","lesson":"Lesson 2 · Expanding and factorising"},{"title":"Grid net","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"14\" y=\"14\" width=\"72\" height=\"72\" fill=\"#e9d7a8\" stroke=\"#5a3b22\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><path d=\"M50 14V86M14 50H86\" stroke=\"#5a3b22\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><text x=\"32\" y=\"38\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">x²</text><text x=\"68\" y=\"38\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">2x</text><text x=\"32\" y=\"74\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">3x</text><text x=\"68\" y=\"74\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">6</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>Double brackets: every term times every term.</b></p><p>(<i>x</i> + 3)(<i>x</i> + 2): the grid cells are <i>x</i>², 2<i>x</i>, 3<i>x</i> and 6, so the answer is <i>x</i>² + 5<i>x</i> + 6.</p><p>Write the signs in the grid too: −2 stays −2.</p>","room":"The Stairwell","lesson":"Lesson 3 · Expanding three brackets"},{"title":"Factor compass","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"50\" r=\"40\" fill=\"#f7efd9\" stroke=\"#8f6a26\" stroke-width=\"8\"/><path d=\"M50 18 L58 50 L50 82 L42 50 Z\" fill=\"#b0412f\"/><path d=\"M50 50 L58 50 L50 82 L42 50 Z\" fill=\"#365a68\"/><circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"50\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#3a2a10\"/></svg>","body":"<p><b>To factorise <i>x</i>² + <i>bx</i> + <i>c</i>:</b> find two numbers that multiply to make <i>c</i> and add to make <i>b</i>.</p><p><i>x</i>² + 7<i>x</i> + 10: 2 × 5 = 10 and 2 + 5 = 7, so it is (<i>x</i> + 2)(<i>x</i> + 5).</p><p>Check by expanding.</p>","room":"The Chart Room","lesson":"Lesson 4 · Simplifying algebraic fractions"},{"title":"Keeper’s logbook","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"14\" y=\"18\" width=\"72\" height=\"64\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#2f4f6b\" stroke=\"#1b2b3a\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"48\" y=\"18\" width=\"4\" height=\"64\" fill=\"#1b2b3a\"/><rect x=\"20\" y=\"26\" width=\"24\" height=\"48\" fill=\"#e9d7a8\"/><rect x=\"56\" y=\"26\" width=\"24\" height=\"48\" fill=\"#e9d7a8\"/><text x=\"32\" y=\"56\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">½</text><text x=\"68\" y=\"56\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">⅓</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>To add or subtract fractions, give them a common denominator first.</b></p><p>1/2 + 1/3 = 3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6</p><p>Change the numerator by the same factor as the denominator: 1/3 = 2/6, because both were multiplied by 2.</p>","room":"The Watch Room","lesson":"Lesson 5 · Adding and subtracting algebraic fractions"},{"title":"Balance scales","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"47\" y=\"22\" width=\"6\" height=\"62\" fill=\"#8f6a26\"/><rect x=\"30\" y=\"84\" width=\"40\" height=\"8\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8f6a26\"/><rect x=\"14\" y=\"24\" width=\"72\" height=\"5\" fill=\"#8f6a26\"/><path d=\"M16 29 L6 52 H30 Z M84 29 L70 52 H94 Z\" fill=\"#c9a35a\" stroke=\"#8f6a26\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></svg>","body":"<p><b>To change the subject, do the same to both sides.</b></p><p>Undo the steps in reverse order. <i>p</i> = 4<i>q</i> − 5: add 5 to both sides, then divide both sides by 4, so <i>q</i> = (<i>p</i> + 5)/4.</p>","room":"The Lens Room","lesson":"Lesson 6 · Changing the subject, harder cases"},{"title":"Odd-and-even lantern","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"34\" y=\"10\" width=\"32\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#3a3a3a\"/><rect x=\"28\" y=\"20\" width=\"44\" height=\"58\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fff1b8\" stroke=\"#3a3a3a\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"49\" r=\"11\" fill=\"#f2c14e\"/><rect x=\"30\" y=\"78\" width=\"40\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#3a3a3a\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"98\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">2n+1</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>Any even number can be written 2<i>n</i>. Any odd number can be written 2<i>n</i> + 1.</b></p><p>Consecutive whole numbers: <i>n</i>, <i>n</i> + 1, <i>n</i> + 2.</p><p>Testing examples suggests a rule; writing it in algebra proves it for every number.</p>","room":"The Gallery","lesson":"Lesson 7 · Algebraic proof"},{"title":"Function machine","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"26\" y=\"26\" width=\"48\" height=\"48\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#9aa5ad\" stroke=\"#5c6670\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"56\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1b2b3a\" font-family=\"Georgia\">f</text><path d=\"M6 50H24M76 50H94\" stroke=\"#3a2a10\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><path d=\"M18 44 L24 50 L18 56 M88 44 L94 50 L88 56\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3a2a10\" stroke-width=\"4\"/></svg>","body":"<p><b>A rule turns each input into one output.</b></p><p>Rule: double, then subtract 3. Input 5 → 5 × 2 = 10 → 10 − 3 = 7.</p><p>In algebra: <i>y</i> = 2<i>x</i> − 3.</p>","room":"The Lantern","lesson":"Lesson 8 · Function notation, domain and range"}]};
